/-!
# The absolute height of a tuple of algebraic numbers

Mathlib's `Height.mulHeight` is the height **relative** to the field it is computed over: over a
number field `K` it is the `[K : ℚ]`-th power of the classical absolute height. This file defines
the absolute height of a tuple of algebraic numbers,

`absMulHeight x = mulHeight_F x ^ (1 / [F : ℚ])` with `F = ℚ(x₀, x₁, …)`,

and proves that the field of definition does not matter: any number field containing the
coordinates computes the same number. It is the tuple analogue of Mathlib's
`NumberField.absMulHeight₁`, which handles a single element through `ℚ⟮x⟯`, and restricts to it.

## Main definitions

* `NumberField.absMulHeight x` for `x : ι → K` over a field `K` of characteristic zero, and
  `NumberField.absLogHeight x`, its logarithm. A tuple with a coordinate that is not algebraic
  gets the junk value `1`, as `NumberField.absMulHeight₁` does.
* `Projectivization.absMulHeight` and `Projectivization.absLogHeight`: the descent to
  `Projectivization K (ι → K)` over a field all of whose elements are algebraic.

## Main results

* `NumberField.absMulHeight_eq`: over a number field `K`, `absMulHeight x` is
  `mulHeight x ^ (finrank ℚ K)⁻¹`. With `NumberField.mulHeight_pow_finrank` of Layer 0.3 this is
  the classical statement that the absolute height does not depend on the field of definition.
* `NumberField.absMulHeight_eq_of_mem`: the same, computed in an intermediate field containing
  the coordinates rather than in `K` itself. This is the form the other results consume.
* `NumberField.absMulHeight_comp`: invariance under an embedding of fields of characteristic
  zero — the absolute height belongs to the algebraic numbers themselves, not to any field
  holding them. It is what lets a value computed over `ℚ` be read off in `ℝ` or `ℂ`.
* `NumberField.absMulHeight_eq_absMulHeight₁`: the absolute height of `![x, 1]` is Mathlib's
  absolute height of `x`, so the tuple definition extends the one-variable one. With it,
  `NumberField.absMulHeight₁_comp` transports Mathlib's `absMulHeight₁` along an embedding —
  an algebraic number and each of its conjugates have the same absolute height.
* `NumberField.absMulHeight_smul_eq`: scaling invariance, for an **algebraic** scalar.
* `NumberField.absMulHeight_comp_le`: reindexing does not raise the height, and
  `NumberField.absMulHeight₁_le_absMulHeight`, the bound on the coordinates of an affine chart
  that Northcott's theorem on projective space runs on.
* `NumberField.absMulHeight_pow` and `NumberField.absMulHeight₁_pow`: the height of a
  coordinatewise power, together with `NumberField.absMulHeight₁_zero` and
  `NumberField.absMulHeight₁_one`. Kronecker's theorem of Layer 1.4 runs on these.

## Implementation notes

⚠ The algebraicity hypothesis on the scalar in `absMulHeight_smul_eq` is not decorative. For `c`
transcendental and `x` a nonzero algebraic tuple, `c • x` has a transcendental coordinate, so its
absolute height is the junk value `1` while that of `x` need not be; `absMulHeight` is then not
constant on the line through `x`. This is refuted among the examples at the end of the file. The
descent to `Projectivization K (ι → K)` is therefore stated over a field with
`[Algebra.IsAlgebraic ℚ K]` — a number field, or `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` — and not over a general
field of characteristic zero, even though `absMulHeight` itself is defined there.

Two facts about `Height.mulHeight` do the work. Passing from the field `ℚ(x₀, x₁, …)` generated by
the coordinates to a larger number field is `NumberField.mulHeight_pow_finrank` of Layer 0.3, and
the normalizing exponent is what cancels the degree it introduces. Passing between two *isomorphic*
number fields is the degree-one case of the same theorem, recorded here as
`NumberField.mulHeight_comp_ringEquiv`; it is what `absMulHeight_comp` needs, since an embedding
carries `ℚ(x₀, x₁, …)` to the field generated by the image coordinates, not into a fixed field.

The index type carries `Finite`, as `Height.mulHeight` does, where the roadmap's pinned signature
has `Fintype`; nothing here sums over `ι`.

## References

E. Bombieri and W. Gubler, *Heights in Diophantine Geometry*, Cambridge University Press (2006),
§1.5. Their `h(P)` is Definition 1.5.4, the absolute *logarithmic* height; the independence of the
field of definition is Lemma 1.5.2, the invariance under coordinate scaling that makes the height
projective is Lemma 1.5.3, and the affine case — the height of `(x₁, …, xₙ)` read as the height of
`(1 : x₁ : ⋯ : xₙ)` — is 1.5.6. `NumberField.absMulHeight_comp` generalizes their Proposition
1.5.17, which states the same invariance for an element of the absolute Galois group of `ℚ`.

This is Layer 0.4 of the `ArithmeticHeights` roadmap.
-/

public section

namespace NumberField

open Function IntermediateField Module

/-!
### The definition

The absolute height is computed over `IntermediateField.adjoin ℚ (Set.range x)`, the field the
coordinates generate, and normalized by the inverse of its degree.
-/

section Def

variable {K : Type*} [Field K] [CharZero K] {ι : Type*} [Finite ι]

/-- The field a tuple of algebraic numbers generates is a number field. Every proof below enters
through this; the definition itself repeats it inline, its exposed body being public. -/
private lemma numberField_adjoin_range {x : ι → K} (hx : ∀ i, IsIntegral ℚ (x i)) :
    NumberField (adjoin ℚ (Set.range x)) :=
  have : FiniteDimensional ℚ (adjoin ℚ (Set.range x)) :=
    finiteDimensional_adjoin fun y hy ↦ by obtain ⟨i, rfl⟩ := hy; exact hx i
  {}

open scoped Classical in
/-- The **absolute multiplicative height** of a tuple of algebraic numbers: the height relative to
the field the coordinates generate, taken to the power `1 / [ℚ(x₀, x₁, …) : ℚ]`. A tuple with a
coordinate that is not algebraic gets the junk value `1`.

This is the tuple analogue of Mathlib's `NumberField.absMulHeight₁`, which takes this route
through `ℚ⟮x⟯`; `absMulHeight_eq_absMulHeight₁` says the two agree where both apply. -/
@[expose] noncomputable def absMulHeight (x : ι → K) : ℝ :=
  if hx : ∀ i, IsIntegral ℚ (x i) then
    haveI : FiniteDimensional ℚ (adjoin ℚ (Set.range x)) :=
      finiteDimensional_adjoin fun y hy ↦ by obtain ⟨i, rfl⟩ := hy; exact hx i
    haveI : NumberField (adjoin ℚ (Set.range x)) := {}
    Height.mulHeight (fun i ↦ (⟨x i, subset_adjoin ℚ _ ⟨i, rfl⟩⟩ : adjoin ℚ (Set.range x)))
      ^ ((finrank ℚ (adjoin ℚ (Set.range x)) : ℝ))⁻¹
  else 1

/-- The **absolute logarithmic height** of a tuple of algebraic numbers. -/
@[expose] noncomputable def absLogHeight (x : ι → K) : ℝ := Real.log (absMulHeight x)

lemma absLogHeight_eq_log_absMulHeight (x : ι → K) :
    absLogHeight x = Real.log (absMulHeight x) :=
  rfl

/-- The definition, with the `NumberField` instance on the generated field taken from the context
instead of rebuilt; every proof below enters through this. -/
private lemma absMulHeight_def {x : ι → K} (hx : ∀ i, IsIntegral ℚ (x i))
    [NumberField (adjoin ℚ (Set.range x))] :
    absMulHeight x =
      Height.mulHeight (fun i ↦ (⟨x i, subset_adjoin ℚ _ ⟨i, rfl⟩⟩ : adjoin ℚ (Set.range x)))
        ^ ((finrank ℚ (adjoin ℚ (Set.range x)) : ℝ))⁻¹ := by
  rw [absMulHeight, dite_eq_left_of_eq_true (eq_true hx)]

open scoped Classical in
/-- The junk value: a tuple with a coordinate that is not algebraic has absolute height `1`. -/
lemma absMulHeight_eq_one_of_not_isIntegral {x : ι → K} (hx : ¬ ∀ i, IsIntegral ℚ (x i)) :
    absMulHeight x = 1 := by
  rw [absMulHeight, dite_eq_right_of_eq_false (eq_false hx)]

end Def

